Steam Frame: a second network path to the same host ("Multi-Link" for a
stream that passes through Nightfall Meteor).

The Steam Frame reaches its PC over the home Wi-Fi and over the wireless
adapter's direct link at once. Steam's own streaming sends its packets over
both and uses whichever copy arrives first. This is the headset's half of the
same for Nightfall; Meteor on the PC is the other half (meteor/src/proxy.rs).

What it changes, all for IPv4 streams only:
- the video and audio sockets listen on every address of the device, not only
  the one the stream started on, so copies sent to the device's address on
  the second link reach them;
- the video and audio pings (every 500 ms, carrying the 16-byte payload the
  host made for this stream) are also sent to the address given to
  LiSetSecondLink(). Meteor recognises the payload and starts sending copies;
- the video receive loop notes which link each packet came over, for the
  counts LiGetSecondLinkStats() returns. The RTP queue already drops a packet
  it has seen, so the first copy to arrive is the one used.

A second-link ping can carry two more bytes (LiSetSecondLinkChoice): the
choice made on the headset (0 copies on both links, 1 the second link first,
2 no second link) and the number of the last pick in Meteor's own menu that
the headset has seen, which is how the two stay in step. They are only sent
to a Meteor that says it understands them.

With "the second link first" the headset itself judges whether that link is
carrying the stream (LiSetSecondLinkWatch): the picture's packets over it, the
holes in their numbering, and the sound's packets as a sign of life when the
screen is still. While the link is not shown to be steady the pings say 3
("the second link first, but both links for now") and Meteor sends copies over
the first link too; a changed verdict is pinged at once, and while the second
link is alone its pings go ten times a second, so that Meteor can tell a dead
link in a fraction of a second as well. Only for a Meteor that says it knows
3 ("link-hold").

The control stream can go over both links as well (LiSetSecondLinkControl):
its ENet socket is no longer tied to the address the stream started on, every
datagram for the host is also sent to its address on the second link (ending
with the stream's 16 bytes and "NFL2", by which Meteor knows the copy and
whose it is), and a datagram that comes from there is handed to ENet as one
from the first (enet/unix.c). ENet numbers what it sends and drops what it has seen, so
either link can go silent without the connection being lost: tried with an
ENet client and server and a relay that cuts each path in turn for 13
seconds, against a 10 second timeout. Only for a Meteor that takes both as
one peer ("control-both").

With no address set (the default) the only difference from upstream is the
wider listening address.

